.TH GIT-TIME 1 "2026-11-25" "git-time 0.3.1" "Git Manual"
.SH NAME
git\-time \- RFC 3161 timestamp evidence for commits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git time [ \-C \fIDIR\fR ] \fIcommand\fR [ \fIarguments\fR ]
.PP
.B git time hook \fIevent\fR [ \fIrest\fR ... ]
.PP
.B git time seal [ \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR ] [ \-\-force ]
.PP
.B git time drop
.PP
.B git time verify [ \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR ] [ \-\-require\-sealed ] [ \-\-require\-signed ] [ \-\-json ] [ \fIcommit\fR ... ]
.br
.B git time verify \-\-openssl [ \-\-require\-sealed ] [ \-\-require\-signed ] \fIcommit\fR
.PP
.B git time log [ \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR ] [ \-\-json ] [ \fIcommit\fR ... ]
.PP
.B git time install [ \-\-accept \fIid\fR=\fIFINGERPRINT\fR ] [ \-\-no\-tsa ]
.PP
.B git time uninstall [ \-\-force ]
.PP
.B git time doctor [ \-\-json ]
.PP
.B git time tsa \fIsubcommand\fR [ \fIarguments\fR ]
.PP
.B git time anchor \fIsubcommand\fR [ \fIarguments\fR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git time
seals commits with RFC 3161 timestamp tokens from one or more timestamp
authorities and stores the tokens inside the commit object as
.B X\-RFC3161\-*
trailers.
Sealing happens from git hooks, in the background, so an ordinary
.B git commit
needs no extra command.
.PP
A seal hashes a pre\-image of the commit, asks every configured authority for
a token over that hash, verifies each answer against the anchor pinned for its
authority, and rewrites the commit with the surviving tokens.
A signed commit is re\-signed over the new bytes.
A commit that cannot be sealed for any reason is left exactly as it was.
.PP
.B git time verify
judges the evidence and fails closed: a missing block, a bad imprint, a token
that does not chain to its pinned anchor, or a broken commit signature is a
failure with exit status 1.
.PP
The commit is only rewritten when it is not yet reachable from a
remote\-tracking ref.
A fast\-forwarded commit is left alone, because rewriting it would put the
branch ahead of its remote and force the next push.
.B git time seal \-\-force
rewrites such a commit when that is wanted.
.PP
An evidence block records what was hashed and which authorities answered.
It is evidence, not trust: whether the tokens are worth anything is decided at
verification time against the pinned anchors.
The format is specified in
.BR docs/format.md .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS hook
.B git time hook \fIevent\fR [ \fIrest\fR ... ]
.PP
The entrypoint the installed shims call.
.I event
is one of
.BR post\-commit ,
.BR post\-merge ,
.B pre\-commit
or
.BR pre\-merge\-commit .
Trailing arguments are passed through unchanged; git passes
.B 1
to
.B post\-merge
for a squash merge.
.PP
A merge never runs
.BR post\-commit ,
which is why both post hooks exist.
The two pre hooks do work only under
.BR "time.gate = pre\-commit" ;
they probe every configured authority over a dummy digest and refuse the
commit before any object is written when fewer than
.B time.required
answers arrive.
A pending drop short\-circuits the probe.
.PP
Shim failures are silent to git, which ignores a
.B post\-commit
status.
Under
.BR "time.on\-failure = fail"
the hook exits 3 instead.
.PP
Exits 0 on success and on every skip, 1 from a refusing gate, 3 on failure
under
.BR "time.on\-failure = fail" ,
and 2 for an unknown event.
.SS seal
.B git time seal [ \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR ] [ \-\-force ]
.PP
Seal HEAD, or every commit of
.I RANGE
oldest first.
Without arguments this is what the hook does, with the outcome printed.
A commit dropped with
.B git time drop
prints
.B "skipped (dropped)"
and is left alone.
.PP
The range form parks HEAD on each commit without moving the index or the
worktree, seals the commits that carry no matching evidence, and restores
HEAD.
Only a range that ends at the branch tip is sealed through the branch; other
members are sealed detached, and the printed object id is where the new
evidence lives.
Descendants of a rewritten member are not repointed.
.TP
.B \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR
A git rev\-range. Every commit in it is sealed, oldest first, in topological
order.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Re\-seal commits that already carry matching evidence, and seal commits that
were dropped on purpose.
.PP
Exits 0 on success and on a skip, 1 if a commit could not be sealed or no
authority answered, and 2 for a bad range or an unreadable configuration.
.SS drop
.B git time drop
.PP
Leave the next commit unsealed on purpose.
The marker survives the shell, so no environment variable has to be
remembered, and
.B verify
reports
.B "UNSEALED (dropped)"
instead of a plain unsealed commit.
Only a hook run that ends in a seal consumes the marker.
.PP
Exits 0, or 2 when the marker cannot be written.
.SS verify
.B git time verify [ \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR ] [ \-\-require\-sealed ] [ \-\-require\-signed ] [ \-\-json ] [ \-\-openssl ] [ \fIcommit\fR ... ]
.PP
Judge the evidence of each commit once, without calling an authority.
The default target is HEAD.
Output is one line per commit: PASS or FAIL, the short object id, the verdict
and the verified quorum, followed by one indented line per authority answer
and per failing gate.
An answer line carries the verdict, the genTime and the signer's serial, so a
PASS shows who answered and when instead of only that a quorum was reached.
The anchor's SHA-256 is the one field left to `--json`, where it is there to be
compared rather than read; in the plain output the same long fingerprint on
every pass drowns the line.
Records follow one another without a gap, and one empty line closes the output.
Commits are judged on a small worker pool and printed in walk order as they
finish, so a long range shows progress; warnings are printed on a PASS too.
.PP
Read\-only, except that the signature check may leave one loose object behind
with
.BR "hash\-object \-w" .
No ref, index or worktree is touched.
.TP
.B \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR
A git rev\-range. Every commit in it is judged, newest first.
.TP
.B \-\-require\-sealed
Fail an unsealed commit.
This is a gate, not a verdict: the verdict stays
.BR unsealed .
.TP
.B \-\-require\-signed
Fail an unsigned commit.
This is a gate, not a verdict.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the findings as one JSON document.
Each
.B per_tsa
entry carries
.BR serial
and
.B anchor
alongside the verdict.
.TP
.B \-\-openssl
Print, and only print, a POSIX
.B sh
script that re\-verifies the tokens of one commit with
.BR openssl .
Nothing is run and no TSA is called; the command exits 0 once the script is
printed.
Exactly one commit has to be named, and only that commit appears in the
script: it is an artifact for one seal, so a range is not accepted and a
missing commit is a usage error.
Conflicts with
.B \-\-json
and
.BR \-\-range .
.PP
The emitted script needs
.B openssl
with its
.B ts
command and nothing else: no
.BR git ,
no
.BR zstd .
It writes only into one
.B mktemp \-d
directory, which it removes again \-\- also when it is interrupted: a signal
stops it with 130/143/129 and only
.B EXIT
cleans up.
Before it recurses, the cleanup checks that the path is absolute and below a
directory, so a lost
.B $scratch
cannot widen into
.B rm \-rf /
or
.BR "rm \-rf ." .
The script carries its payload: the pre\-image of the commit is printed into it
as clear text and the tokens follow base64\-encoded, so it does not read the
repository and a reader sees the commit being judged.
Keep it next to the commit it names.
A message that is not UTF\-8 falls back to base64 for the whole pre\-image.
The embedded pre\-image is hashed and compared with the block's
.B X\-RFC3161\-Imprint
before any token is trusted.
Each token is decoded and checked with
.B openssl ts \-verify \-purpose timestampsign
against the anchor of the authority.
The anchor is looked up at
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git-time/anchors/<id>.pem
(or
.BR $HOME/.config/git-time/anchors/<id>.pem ),
then at the path the generating machine had, and the bundle is passed whole
as
.BR \-CAfile ,
so its intermediates count as trust material.
It prints PASS or FAIL per token and a summary, and exits 1 when anything
failed.
Use an OpenSSL rather than the LibreSSL that ships as
.B /usr/bin/openssl
on macOS: its
.B ts
is older, has no
.B \-purpose
and refuses SHA\-384/SHA\-512 tokens.
With such a binary the script still runs, names the version in a NOTE and
skips the EKU check instead of passing it silently.
A token whose authority has no anchor names both paths it looked at and fails.
A commit without a token is summarised as SKIP, so an unsealed or dropped
commit stays visible.
The two require gates were applied when the script was generated and are named
in it, but the script does not re\-check them.
Only tokens in the commit trailer are audited: a seal kept only in a note is
not reached.
.TP
.B \fIcommit\fR ...
Additional commits to judge, on top of any range.
An argument that does not name a commit is a usage error.
.PP
The verdicts are sealed, under\-quorum, none, unsealed, bad\-imprint,
bad\-token, untrusted, bad\-sig and unsigned.
.B none
is a block that asked nobody, because no authority was configured when it was
sealed.
Exits 0 only when every commit passes, 1 on any evidence or gate failure, and
2 for a usage or configuration problem.
.SS log
.B git time log [ \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR ] [ \-\-json ] [ \fIcommit\fR ... ]
.PP
List the evidence of each commit, newest first, as one positional line: short
object id, authority ids, the genTime of every valid token, and the verdict.
An empty column is
.BR \- .
Lines are printed as a small worker pool judges the commits, still in walk
order, so a long range shows progress instead of a blank screen.
A record is one line, so nothing separates them and no empty line closes the
run.
.PP
.B log
is a record, not a judgement: it exits 0 whenever it could read everything
and 1 only for a commit it could not read, or 141 when the reader stopped the
stream early.
Use
.B verify
to gate CI.
The JSON form is collected rather than streamed, and carries no
.BR pass ,
.B gates
or
.BR required ,
but its
.B per_tsa
entries keep
.BR serial
and
.BR anchor .
.TP
.B \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR
A git rev\-range. Every commit in it is listed, newest first.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the evidence as one JSON document.
.TP
.B \fIcommit\fR ...
Additional commits to list.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 for a commit that could not be read, and 2 for a usage or
configuration problem.
.SS install
.B git time install [ \-\-accept \fIid\fR=\fIFINGERPRINT\fR ] [ \-\-no\-tsa ]
.PP
Wire the hooks into the current repository.
.B post\-commit
and
.B post\-merge
are always wired;
.B pre\-commit
and
.B pre\-merge\-commit
are wired only under
.BR "time.gate = pre\-commit" .
The hooks directory is the one git reports, so a
.B core.hooksPath
set by husky or the pre\-commit framework is honoured.
.PP
A foreign hook at any of those names is preserved as
.I name\fB.pre\-time\fR
(then
.B name.pre\-time.n)
and chained, never overwritten.
The shims run a preserved hook first and propagate its failure.
.PP
The authority set belongs to the repository, not to a flag: an existing
.B time.tsa.*
configuration is kept unchanged, and an empty one hands over to
.B git time tsa select
when stdin is a terminal.
A failed anchor fetch is a warning, not a failure; the authority stays
configured so
.B doctor
can name the gap.
.PP
A rotated root is refused unless
.B \-\-accept
names it, because the fetched root of a pinned authority is compared with the
published value.
.B install
pins several authorities, so the value is
.IR id = fingerprint
and repeatable; a bare fingerprint is accepted only when exactly one authority
is in play, configured or being written.
The accepted root is appended next to the retired one, never over it, and
becomes the first pin when nothing was pinned for that id.
.PP
The hooks resolve
.B git\-time
as a bare command through
.BR PATH .
The binary must be on
.B PATH
for any sealing to happen; otherwise the shims exit 0 and every commit stays
unsealed and silent.
.B install
warns when the binary is not found, and
.B doctor
reports it as a problem.
.TP
.B \-\-accept \fIid\fR=\fIFINGERPRINT\fR
Accept the root of a rotation. Repeatable. A bare fingerprint is taken
only when exactly one authority is in play, configured or being written. Under
.B \-\-no\-tsa
nothing is fetched and the value is parsed but unused.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tsa
Write no authority configuration; keep whatever is configured.
.PP
A mismatch or an unreachable authority on the keep path is only a warning, so
the status stays 0 there. Exits 1 for an anchor that cannot be pinned in the
handover, and 2 on a usage or configuration error, including no authority
configured while stdin is not a terminal.
.SS uninstall
.B git time uninstall [ \-\-force ]
.PP
Remove every shim this tool owns, all four names included, and restore each
preserved hook by rename, so its bytes and mode come back exactly.
A hook that is not ours is left alone.
Running it twice succeeds.
.PP
A shim edited by hand is named and left in place.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Also replace a hand\-edited shim, keeping the edit under the next preserved
name next to the restored original.
When nothing was ever preserved, the edited shim stays where it is.
.PP
Exits 0, or 2 on a configuration problem.
.SS doctor
.B git time doctor [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Report, one fact per line, where the binary is, which hooks directory git
uses, the state of each of the four hooks
.B ( ours ,
.BR chained ,
.BR foreign ,
.BR absent ,
.BR not\-executable ,
.BR dangling ),
whether
.B time.gate
agrees with the installed pre hooks, how many authorities are configured, and
each anchor path with its SHA\-256 fingerprint.
Each problem is repeated as a
.B problem:
line.
.PP
A missing binary, no authority, a missing, unreadable or unparseable anchor,
an anchor pinned without a comparison, a gate mismatch, an unknown gate value,
a shim or preserved hook without its execute bit, and a dangling symlink all
exit 1.
A foreign or absent hook alone does not.
An anchor pinned without a comparison is reported with the command that records
the comparison; where its bundle is gone, the command names the fetch that pins
it again first.
A
.B tofu
value outside
.B true
or
.B false
is a configuration error.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the report as one JSON object. Its
.B problems
array decides the exit status.
.PP
Exits 0 when healthy, 1 on any problem, and 2 on a usage or configuration
error.
.SS tsa
.B git time tsa \fIsubcommand\fR [ \fIarguments\fR ]
.PP
The authority registry.
An authority is a
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR
section of the local repository configuration.
An id is restricted to
.BR a\-z ,
.B 0\-9
and
.BR \- ;
when
.B \-\-id
is absent it is derived from the URL host, so
.B https://tsa.example.net/tsr
becomes
.BR tsa\-example\-net .
.SS "tsa add"
.B git time tsa add \fIurl\fR [ \-\-id \fIID\fR ] [ \-\-anchor \fIPATH\fR ] [ \-\-alg \fIsha256\fR|\fIsha512\fR ] [ \-\-policy \fIOID\fR ] [ \-\-fingerprint \fIFP\fR ] [ \-\-timeout \fIDURATION\fR ] [ \-\-insecure\-http ]
.PP
Register an authority.
Every value is validated before the first key is written, so a refused add
leaves no half section behind.
An already configured id is refused.
A
.B http://
URL needs
.BR \-\-insecure\-http .
.TP
.B \-\-id \fIID\fR
The config id.
.TP
.B \-\-anchor \fIPATH\fR
The PEM bundle pinned for this id.
Defaults to
.B \fIanchors\-dir\fR/\fIid\fR.pem.
.TP
.B \-\-alg \fIsha256\fR|\fIsha512\fR
The imprint algorithm and the algorithm requested from the authority.
Default
.BR sha256 .
.TP
.B \-\-policy \fIOID\fR
A TSA policy OID to request.
.TP
.B \-\-fingerprint \fIFP\fR
The root fingerprint you expect, 20 or 32 colon\-separated hex octets in any
case.
It is stored in
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.fingerprint
and compared by the next pin;
.B add
itself fetches nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-timeout \fIDURATION\fR
Per\-attempt timeout. Default
.BR 6s .
.TP
.B \-\-insecure\-http
Accept a plain
.B http://
endpoint and warn that the transport is unprotected.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 for an existing id, and 2 for a bad URL, id, fingerprint, duration
or policy.
.SS "tsa list"
.B git time tsa list [ \-\-json ]
.PP
List each configured authority with its URL, algorithm, anchor path, the
newest root's fingerprint and expiry date, anchor state and whether it is
plain http.
Every root of the bundle follows on an indented
.B root
line.
Where the bundle is missing and the id is a preset, the row falls back to the
last cached probe answer, in the selector's
.B fetch <date>
word, and then to the date that preset measured, in the
.B listed
word.
A plain listing opens no socket.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the listing as one JSON array.
The
.B not_after_source
field says which source answered
.RB ( bundle ,
.BR probe ,
.B preset
or null).
.PP
Exits 0, or 2 on a configuration error.
.SS "tsa remove"
.B git time tsa remove \fIid\fR
.PP
Delete the whole
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR
section. The anchor file is not removed.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 for an unknown id, and 2 for a bad id or configuration error.
.SS "tsa preset"
.B git time tsa preset list [ \-\-refresh ]
.PP
Print the built\-in presets and every configured authority that carries a
url, with their endpoint, algorithm, scope
.B ( default ,
.B opt\-in
or
.BR custom ),
the newest fingerprint they publish
.RB ( tofu
only for a row that publishes none), the newest root's expiry date, and
.B anchored
or
.BR missing .
A
.B custom
row is a
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR
section no built\-in row covers, and it is listed after the built\-ins.
Nothing is published for it, so the listing never fetches it: its date comes
from its own bundle, reading
.B tofu
until that bundle is pinned and
.B EXPIRED
once every root in it is past.
Pin it with
.BR "git time anchor fetch \fIurl\fR \-\-id \fIid\fR [ \-\-insecure\-http ]" ,
which prints the fingerprint in full.
The date carries
.B (expires soon)
in its last 90 days and
.B EXPIRED
past it; three sources answer it in this order: the pinned bundle, then a
cached probe answer in the words
.B tsa select
uses and judged against the one instant the listing took, then the date the
preset table measured for the row, in a word of its own,
.BR "listed \fIdate\fR" ,
.BR "listed expires \fIdate\fR"
or
.BR "listed EXPIRED" .
The table is only the last word, and
.B \-
is left where even it has nothing; its dates are re\-measured with
.B \-\-refresh
at release, and a row within a year of its date is rotated or dropped before
the next tag.
A pinned row whose authority serves a root the local bundle does not hold
appends
.BR ! :
that authority has rotated, and the answer is a review.
A plain run only ever reads, so it never prints
.BR ? ;
\fB?\fR and
.B …
come from
.BR \-\-refresh ,
the latter only when an answer arrives too late to be read, and both only
among the rows with no local pin.
A custom row publishes nothing, so neither is ever queued for it.
A pinned row keeps its pin date throughout and never trades it for the served
one; an answer naming a foreign root marks it as above.
.B not pinned
is the wording for a built\-in that publishes no root, and no built\-in is in
that position any more.
.PP
A plain run opens no socket at all: it paints the rows that answered from the
cache and nothing is queued.
A row that failed or served no published root and has no entry keeps
.B \-
here; the next
.B \-\-refresh
asks it again, and a marker is read from the cache whenever the reply names no
root.
The cache is shared with
.BR "tsa select" .
Either way the cache is the only write: a probe pins nothing and writes no
anchor.
Exits 0.
.TP
.B \-\-refresh
Ask every authority that publishes a root and wait for the queue to drain
before printing.
Nothing is dropped first, so a refresh that fails leaves the previous date in
place until it ages out, and the
.B !\&
marker already read from that entry stays with it.
A row with no local pin that cannot answer prints
.BR ? ;
a pinned row keeps its pin date.
.PP
.B git time tsa preset add \fIname\fR [ \-\-accept \fIid\fR=\fIFINGERPRINT\fR ]
.PP
Configure one preset, anchor included, through the same fetch path
.B install
uses.
A root fingerprint that does not match the published value is a refusal, so a
pinned preset never accepts a substituted root; when the publication really
moved, compare the value out of band and repeat with
.BR \-\-accept ,
bare or carrying the preset's own id — any other id matches nothing.
The report names the accepted root and its date.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 when no anchor could be pinned, and 2 for an unknown name, an
unparseable
.B --accept
value, or
configuration error.
.SS "tsa select"
.B git time tsa select [ \-\-interactive ] [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.PP
Choose interactively which authorities this repository uses, pin the chosen
anchors that are built in, and remove the sections that were not chosen.
Every configured authority that carries a url is a row: the built\-ins, then a
configured id no built\-in covers, which is listed after them and says
.B custom
where a scope is shown.
An unticked one loses its section too, though the bundle it names stays on
disk; an unknown section with no url cannot be enabled and is left alone.
Saving with nothing selected is therefore the removal path.
A section that names only a url is still a row, but it cannot seal until it
also names an
.B anchor
because the policy refuses a section without one;
.B "git time tsa add"
writes both keys. A section in a wider config scope (machine-wide) cannot be
removed with
.BR --local :
unticking it says so and leaves the section there, and the rest of the answer
still applies.
.PP
The rows of the authorities already configured start ticked, so entering the
selector to change one keeps the others; an unconfigured row starts clear.
A custom row is kept, not pinned again, because there is no published root to
compare against, so its own bundle stays exactly as it is.
Pin it with
.BR "git time anchor fetch \fIurl\fR \-\-id \fIid\fR [ \-\-insecure\-http ]" ,
which prints the fingerprint in full.
.B n
clears every tick, and saving that empty selection is how sections are
removed.
.PP
The list is a cursor interface:
.B space
ticks a row,
.B a
and
.B n
take or clear all,
.B j
and
.B k
or the arrow keys move,
.B r
refreshes,
.B enter
saves and pins the built-in anchors among the ticks,
.B q
aborts.
Type a selection
.B ( 1,3\-4 ,
.BR all ,
.BR none )
to override the ticks; an empty answer keeps them, and that is also what
.B enter
saves when nothing was typed.
.PP
The \fBpin\fR column is the trust tier and shows the newest root's expiry;
one in its last 90 days reads
.BR "pin expires \fIdate\fR" ,
a bundle whose every root is past reads
.BR EXPIRED ,
and a published root not pinned on this machine reads the date the table
measured for it, in a word of its own,
.BR "listed \fIdate\fR" ,
.BR "listed expires \fIdate\fR"
or
.BR "listed EXPIRED" ;
.B enter
pins it. The bundle, then a probe, then the table, so nothing measured ever
outranks a local fact. A root that disagrees with the table is a mismatch and
a refusal.
.B tofu
is a custom row's tier until its own bundle is pinned: nothing was published
to compare, so the first pin can only be reviewed through the fingerprint
.B anchor fetch
prints, and once a bundle is there the row reads its date like any other, or
.B EXPIRED
once every root in it is past.
A live terminal replaces that wording with
.B …
and then the served date, so
.B listed
is what the line prompt keeps.
.PP
Every authority that publishes a root is also probed in the background, four at
a time, and the column fills itself in; a row that publishes nothing is never
probed, because no reply could match it, and keeps
.BR tofu .
Where nothing is pinned here the answer stands in for a pin: the newest root
the authority served, being
.BR "fetch \fIdate\fR" ,
.BR "fetch expires \fIdate\fR"
inside its last 90 days, or
.BR "fetch EXPIRED"
past it.
.B fetch
is a word of its own, so a served date is never read as a pin; a probe pins
nothing and writes no anchor, and its cache entry is all it leaves behind.
A queued or in\-flight row reads
.BR … ;
\fB?\fR marks a probe that failed or a reply holding no root the preset publishes,
and the footer counts the first kind
.BR "probe failed: \fIn\fR"
and names the rows behind the second as
.BR "served root not published: \fIids\fR" .
A pin always keeps the date, and a served root the local bundle does not hold
appends
.BR ! :
that authority has rotated, and the answer is a review, not a replacement.
.PP
An answer is kept for seven days at
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git\-time/probe/<id>
(or
.BR ~/.config/git\-time/probe/<id> ),
so a second run paints the rows that answered from the cache and opens no
socket for them; a row that failed or served no published root and has no entry
is asked again.
A date can be up to a week old.
.B r
asks again without dropping anything first, so a failed refresh keeps the
previous answer until it ages out, and a row with no answer of its own keeps
that answer's
.B !\&
marker, so a network blip does not hide a rotation.
.B tsa preset list
reads the same cache and
.B \-\-refresh
is its one fetching path.
.PP
Without a terminal the command refuses rather than hanging.
.TP
.B \-\-interactive
Prompt even when stdin is not a terminal, so a piped answer works.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be enabled, kept and disabled, change nothing.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 when a chosen anchor cannot be pinned, and 2 without a terminal,
after three unparseable answers, or on a configuration error.
.SS "tsa restore"
.B git time tsa restore [ \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR ] [ \-\-accept \fIid\fR=\fIFINGERPRINT\fR ] [ \fIcommit\fR ... ]
.PP
Backfill the anchors a clone lacks, from the authority ids the evidence names.
A preset is fetched and pinned when its published root is one the blocks name;
a fetched root the blocks do not name is a rotation that needs an
.BR \-\-accept ,
and is otherwise reported and left unpinned.
The signature state of every commit that named an id is printed before the
fetch, so a reader sees which blocks were unverified when they chose.
.PP
An id that is not a preset carries no URL anywhere and is named with the
.B tsa add
and
.B anchor fetch
steps instead.
.PP
An authority whose pinned bundle already holds a root the blocks name is
skipped, and the summary counts it.
.TP
.B \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR
A git rev\-range. Without it and without a
.I commit
argument, every commit reachable from HEAD is read.
.TP
.B \-\-accept \fIid\fR=\fIFINGERPRINT\fR
Take a root the blocks do not name. Repeatable; a bare fingerprint is
accepted only when exactly one authority is in play.
.TP
.I commit ...
Commits to read; without a range they are the only source.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 when a pin is refused or an id has no preset, and 2 on a usage or
configuration error.
.SS anchor
.B git time anchor \fIsubcommand\fR [ \fIarguments\fR ]
.PP
Work with pinned anchor certificates.
Every self\-signed certificate of a bundle is a trust anchor for its authority;
a bundle with no root is unusable.
.SS "anchor fetch"
.B git time anchor fetch \fIurl\fR \-\-id \fIid\fR [ \-\-insecure\-http ] [ \-\-accept \fIFINGERPRINT\fR ]
.PP
Fetch a PEM, DER or PKCS#7 bundle and pin it for
.BR id .
For a preset, its issuer chain is appended behind the root.
The bundle is parsed before it replaces anything, so a garbled fetch leaves a
working anchor intact.
The target is the configured
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.anchor
when set, else the anchors directory.
.PP
A root a preset does not publish and that is not pinned yet is a rotation: the
fetch refuses, prints both fingerprints and writes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-id \fIid\fR
The authority the bundle belongs to. Required.
.TP
.B \-\-insecure\-http
Accept a plain
.B http://
URL.
.TP
.B \-\-accept \fIFINGERPRINT\fR
Append the fetched root, whose fingerprint the refusal printed.
The fetch addresses one id, so the value is bare.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 for an unusable bundle or a refused rotation, and 2 for a bad id,
URL or configuration error.
A bundle for an id without a section is written and warned about as an
orphan.
.PP
A first pin with nothing to compare has no preset and no
.B tsa add \-\-fingerprint
value behind it, so it is trust on first use: the fetched root is printed in
full and
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.tofu
is set.
A re\-fetch of the same root keeps the mark, and a match against a stored
expectation or a publication, or an
.BR \-\-accept ,
clears it.
.BR "git time anchor confirm \fIid\fR"
records the out\-of\-band comparison the mark is waiting for.
The mark changes no verdict: it fails
.B doctor
and warns at seal time only.
To have an unconfirmed pin count, raise
.B time.required
so the authority's answer is needed for the quorum and set
.BR "time.on\-failure = fail" ,
which turns a short seal into exit 3 where a wrapper, not git, sees it.
.SS "anchor confirm"
.B git time anchor confirm \fIid\fR [ \fIFINGERPRINT\fR ]
.PP
Record that a trust\-on\-first\-use pin was compared with the authority's own
publication, and clear
.BR time.tsa.\fIid\fR.tofu .
Without a fingerprint every root of the bundle is confirmed and printed; with
one, the fingerprint must be a root of the bundle or nothing is cleared.
A pin that was never marked says so and still exits 0.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 for an unreadable bundle or a fingerprint it does not carry, and 2
for a bad id or fingerprint.
.SS "anchor list"
.B git time anchor list [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Name each configured anchor with its SHA\-256 fingerprint and
.BR notAfter .
A past
.B notAfter
is a cheap stale signal.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the listing as one JSON array.
.PP
Exits 0, or 2 on a configuration error.
.SS "anchor pin"
.B git time anchor pin [ \-\-check ]
.PP
Write
.B time.anchors\-lock
in the repository root, one
.I id fingerprint
line per root of every configured authority, or compare the anchors against it.
The file is sorted by id and fingerprint, so a rotation moves the retired line
down and adds a line for the new root, instead of updating a value in place
that nobody can review.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Compare instead of writing, naming every missing, unparseable, added, removed
and unrecorded id.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 on a mismatch, an unreadable lock or an unusable anchor, and 2 on a
configuration error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-C \fIDIR\fR
Run in the repository at
.I DIR
instead of the current directory.
Accepted by every command.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version with the copyright line, the licence and the source URL, then
exit.
.SH "X\-RFC3161 EVIDENCE"
A sealed commit carries a trailing message paragraph of
.B X\-RFC3161\-
lines:
.B Imprint
(what was hashed),
.B Object
(provenance),
.B Token
(one per authority that answered) and
.B Fail
(one per authority that was asked and did not deliver).
Tokens are zstd\-compressed and base64url\-encoded.
The block travels inside the object and is covered by the commit signature.
.PP
The block is evidence, not trust.
Verification recomputes the pre\-image, compares the imprint in constant time,
and judges every token on its own imprint algorithm and id against the anchor
pinned for that id.
.PP
The format, the pre\-image rules, the budget and the mandatory verifier checks
are specified in
.BR docs/format.md .
.SH CONFIGURATION
All keys are git configuration, normally repository\-local.
An unknown value for a key with a fixed vocabulary is an error with exit 2,
never a silent fallback to the default.
Durations accept
.BR 500ms ,
.B 10s ,
.B 3m
and bare seconds.
A relative anchor path is resolved against the repository;
.B ~/
is resolved against
.BR HOME .
.PP
An authority whose anchor bundle does not load is left out of the seal and
recorded as a
.B Fail
for its id, unless failures are not recorded.
Only when no configured authority has a usable anchor does the seal refuse.
.TP
.B time.required
Default
.BR 1 .
Distinct authorities that must answer for a seal to count as sealed.
.B 0
makes the seal best effort.
.TP
.B time.deadline
Default
.BR 10s .
Hard bound on the whole fan\-out, not on one authority.
.TP
.B time.on\-failure
Default
.BR warn .
.B warn
prints and exits 0;
.B fail
prints and exits 3.
.TP
.B time.gate
Default
.BR post\-commit .
.B post\-commit
seals after the object exists;
.B pre\-commit
probes the authorities first and refuses the commit on a short quorum.
.TP
.B time.store
Default
.BR trailer .
Where evidence goes:
.B trailer
(inside the object),
.B note
.B ( refs/notes/rfc3161 )
or
.BR both .
.TP
.B time.seal\-during\-rebase
Default
.BR all .
Which rewritten commits a rebase seals:
.BR all ,
only the
.B tip
with the exact command for the rest printed once, or
.B off
with the
.B seal \-\-range
hint.
.TP
.B time.record\-failures
Default
.BR true .
Whether a failed authority leaves an
.B X\-RFC3161\-Fail
line.
.TP
.B time.enabled
Default
.BR true .
Whether the hook seals at all.
.TP
.B time.max\-trailer\-bytes
Default
.BR 65536 .
Budget for the trailer block.
A breach drops the largest token and records
.BR oversized .
.TP
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.url
The endpoint requests are posted to.
Required for every authority.
.TP
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.anchor
The pinned PEM bundle tokens are judged against.
Required for every authority.
.TP
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.alg
Default
.BR sha256 .
Imprint and request algorithm:
.B sha256
or
.BR sha512 .
.TP
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.policy
Unset by default.
A policy OID to request, validated before it is written.
.TP
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.fingerprint
Unset by default.
The root the next pin expects, 20 or 32 colon\-separated hex octets in any
case; a different root is refused.
Written by
.BR "git time tsa add \-\-fingerprint" .
.TP
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.tofu
Unset by default.
Set when a pin had nothing to compare; cleared by
.BR "git time anchor confirm" .
It changes no verdict, so
.B doctor
reports it and every marked authority shares one warning line at seal time.
.TP
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.timeout
Default
.BR 6s .
Per\-attempt timeout.
.TP
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.insecure\-http
Default
.BR false .
Records that a plain
.B http://
endpoint was accepted on purpose.
All such authorities share one warning line at seal time.
.PP
.B time.anchors\-lock
is a file, not a key.
It records reviewed fingerprints but is not a trust store: verification keeps
judging against the pinned bundles, so deleting the lock changes no verdict.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I anchors\-dir
The pinned bundles, by default
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git\-time/anchors
or
.BR ~/.config/git\-time/anchors .
Self\-signed certificates are the anchors.
An older reader takes the first certificate as the only anchor; the block's
.B X\-RFC3161\-Anchor
line narrows which root is used.
.TP
.I probe\-dir
The probe cache, by default
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git\-time/probe
or
.BR ~/.config/git\-time/probe ,
one
.I id
file per authority.
A line of day, fetch second and fingerprint, dropped after seven days; not a
trust store, and deleting it only means asking again.
.TP
.B time.anchors\-lock
In the repository root, written by
.BR "anchor pin" .
One
.I id fingerprint
line per root of every configured authority.
.TP
.I gitdir\fB/time\-skip
The marker written by
.BR drop .
It is removed by the next hook run that seals.
.TP
.I gitdir\fB/time\-dropped
One dropped object id per line, so
.B seal
can report a deliberate drop.
Capped at 200 entries and pruned as commits disappear.
.TP
.B hooks\-dir/{post\-commit,post\-merge,pre\-commit,pre\-merge\-commit}
The shims.
Each runs a preserved hook first, then
.BR "git\-time hook" .
.TP
.I hooks\-dir\fB/\fIname\fB.pre\-time
The foreign hooks displaced by
.BR install ,
restored by
.BR uninstall .
Further displacements take
.B name.pre\-time.n\&.
.TP
.I rebase\-dir\fB/time\-hinted
Set once per rebase when
.B time.seal\-during\-rebase = off
so the hint is printed once.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success.
A skip is a success.
.TP
.B 1
Evidence or seal failure, and the verdict of a refusing gate.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or configuration problem.
.TP
.B 3
Only from
.BR hook ,
and only under
.BR "time.on\-failure = fail" .
git ignores a
.B post\-commit
status; a wrapper does not.
.TP
.B 141
Only from a streamed run that stopped being read —
.BR log ,
.B verify
— when the reader closes the pipe early, as
.B head
does.
SIGPIPE keeps its default disposition, so the process ends quietly instead of
reporting a broken pipe.
It can land in place of the 1 the run would have had, and it is a race: a run
that finishes before the reader closes still exits its own status, and a usage
error exits 2 before any output is piped.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set a repository up:
.PP
.B git time tsa select
.PP
.B git time install
.PP
.B git commit \-m \&"add entry point\&"
.PP
Judge the history in CI:
.PP
.B git time verify \-\-range origin/main..HEAD \-\-require\-sealed
.PP
Seal a range after the fact, and keep the printed ids:
.PP
.B git time seal \-\-range HEAD~5..HEAD
.PP
Review anchors, then catch later changes:
.PP
.B git time anchor pin
.PP
.B git time anchor pin \-\-check
.PP
Skip one commit on purpose:
.PP
.B git time drop
.PP
.B git commit \-m \&"wip\&"
.SH ROTATION
An authority rotates when a key gets old, a root expires or one leaks.
The history that authority already sealed must keep verifying, and a new root
must not enter trust by itself.
A fetch therefore keeps the old bundle and refuses a root it does not expect:
.PP
.nf
.RS
git time anchor: the root fetched for `tsa-example-net` from
https://tsa.example.net/tsr does not match: expected \fIold\fR, got \fInew\fR;
the bundle was not pinned and the existing anchor is untouched
git time anchor: pass `--accept \fInew\fR` to append it next to the pinned root
.RE
.fi
.PP
Compare the fetched value with the fingerprint the authority publishes out of
band, then repeat the command with
.BR \-\-accept .
.B anchor fetch
addresses one id, so the value is bare.
.BR tsa " " preset " " add
names one too, and takes the bare value as well.
.B install
and
.B tsa restore
may address several authorities and take
.BI \-\-accept " id=fingerprint" ,
repeatable; a bare value needs exactly one authority in play.
.PP
The accepted root is written ahead of the retired one, never over it; for an id
with nothing pinned it is the first pin instead.
Each root only vouches for tokens inside its own validity window, and the
verifier intersects the pinned roots with the fingerprints the block names, so
a new root never blesses an old token and an old root never vouches for a new
one.
The block names the roots that were valid at the token's genTime and is covered
by the commit signature.
.PP
The append adds one line per root to
.BR time.anchors\-lock .
The file is sorted by id and fingerprint, so the retired line moves down and
appears again after the line for the new root.
Review the diff and commit it with the rotation: it is the record of the
decision.
.B anchor pin \-\-check
reports the same change as
.B added
and
.B removed
lines and exits 1 until the lock is updated, which is what makes a rotation
visible in CI.
.PP
.B doctor
names a rotation twice:
.B "has no root valid now"
on an anchor whose every pinned root has expired, and a fingerprint the commit
at HEAD names but this machine has not pinned.
Both lines carry the command that brings the root.
.PP
On a clone that lacks the root,
.B verify
reports UNTRUSTED with
.B anchor\-not\-pinned
and points at
.BR "tsa restore" ,
which reads the ids and named roots out of the evidence and takes the new root
only with an
.BR \-\-accept .
.PP
An older
.B git\-time
still decodes the block, but it does not know the
.B X\-RFC3161\-Anchor
line: it ignores it and takes the first certificate of the pinned bundle as
.B "the anchor,"
the way this feature's predecessors did.
The fresh root is written first, so that reader verifies evidence sealed under
the new root and fails old blocks that name the retired root.
A current
.B git\-time
reads the line, picks the named root and verifies both.
Keep the retired root in the bundle and in the lock while older readers have to
keep checking; otherwise upgrading the reader is the fix.
.SH "SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS"
The pinned anchors are the trust store.
A token is only as good as the bundle pinned under its id, so pin deliberately
and review a changed fingerprint out of band against the authority's own
publication.
The lock is a review artifact, not a trust store.
A first pin of a custom authority that had no expected fingerprint is marked
.BR tofu :
it may be whatever the network handed back, so check it against the authority's
own publication and
.BR "git time anchor confirm \fIid\fR \fIfingerprint\fR" .
The mark changes no verdict, and
.B doctor
keeps naming it until it is cleared.
.PP
Plain
.B http://
presets keep full token integrity, because the CMS signature is checked
against the pinned root.
Transport only exposes the imprint and lets an attacker block the authority.
That is why every insecure authority warns at seal time.
.PP
Published history is never rewritten by the hook.
Rewriting a commit a remote already has moves the branch ahead of it and can
force the next push.
Sealing it is an explicit
.B seal \-\-force
decision.
.PP
The hooks resolve
.B git\-time
through
.BR PATH ,
with git's environment and no shell aliases.
Whichever binary comes first on
.B PATH
is what runs at commit time.
.PP
Verification fails closed, and
.B verify
exits 0 only when every commit passes.
A token must chain to its anchor at
.BR genTime ,
must carry a critical extended key usage of exactly
.B id\-kp\-timeStamping
and nothing else (RFC 3161, section 2.3), and must lie in the accepted
genTime window.
